\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Primitive invariants and their quadratic form}

\related{\ref{ca-000J}}

\p{Let #{\mathfrak g} be finite-dimensional complex semisimple of
rank #{\ell}. Write
#{I=(\bigwedge\mathfrak g)^{\mathfrak g}} for the adjoint invariants
and #{I^+} for their positive-degree part. Extend the Killing form
#{\kappa} to the determinant pairing #{\langle-,-\rangle} on each
exterior degree, with different degrees orthogonal. Let
#{\pi:\bigwedge\mathfrak g\to I} be the invariant projection along
#{\mathfrak g\mathbin{\cdot}\bigwedge\mathfrak g}, the span of all
adjoint derivatives. The coproduct on #{I} is

##{\Delta=(\pi\otimes\pi)\Delta_0|_I,}

where #{\Delta_0(x)=x\otimes1+1\otimes x} for #{x\in\mathfrak g}.
It is this projected coproduct that defines primitive invariants:

##{P=\{p\in I:\Delta p=p\otimes1+1\otimes p\}
=I^+\cap((I^+)^2)^\perp.}

The ordinary exterior coproduct need not take #{I} into #{I\otimes I}.
The Lie-homology construction makes #{I}, with the displayed
#{\Delta}, a graded super Hopf algebra; multiplication on
#{I\otimes I} uses the Koszul rule
#{(a\otimes b)(c\otimes d)=(-1)^{\lvert b\rvert\lvert c\rvert}ac\otimes bd}
for homogeneous elements. The coproduct is dual to the wedge
product. This is the input from
\citet{Theorem 10.1 and Proposition 10.11, pp. 240--241}{meinrenken2013clifford}.}

\p{The Hopf--Koszul--Samelson theorem says that inclusion extends to

##{\bigwedge P\xrightarrow{\sim}I.}

Here every primitive has odd exterior degree. Indeed, a nonzero
homogeneous even primitive #{p} would have a least nilpotent power
#{p^k=0}; the component #{k p\otimes p^{k-1}} of #{\Delta(p^k)}
could not vanish in characteristic zero. The orthogonal decomposition
#{I^+=P\oplus(I^+)^2} then proves generation: substituting it
repeatedly expresses every element as a polynomial in #{P}, since
the augmentation ideal is nilpotent. For independence, contraction
by a primitive acts as an odd derivation on #{I}, and contraction
of two primitives is a scalar. The first assertion is dual to
#{\Delta} preserving multiplication; the second follows from
#{P\perp(I^+)^2}. Successive contractions by a dual primitive basis
extract the coefficients of an exterior-monomial relation, starting
with the largest number of factors. All coefficients vanish.
This proves the stated isomorphism; compare
\citet{Theorem 10.2 and Proposition 10.13, pp. 242--243}{meinrenken2013clifford}.
By \ref{fcap-003P}, #{\dim I=2^\ell}, so #{\dim P=\ell}.}

\p{For #{x\in\mathfrak g}, let #{\iota_x} be contraction using
#{\kappa}, and set
#{\iota_{x_1\wedge\cdots\wedge x_k}=\iota_{x_1}\cdots\iota_{x_k}}.
Exterior reversion is
#{\operatorname{rev}(u)=(-1)^{k(k-1)/2}u} on degree #{k}.
The form relevant to Clifford multiplication is

##{B_\wedge(u,v)=(\iota_u v)_0
=\langle\operatorname{rev}(u),v\rangle,\qquad
Q_P(p)=B_\wedge(p,p).}

In particular, on primitive degree #{2m+1},
#{B_\wedge(p,q)=(-1)^m\langle p,q\rangle}. The Clifford polar
relation for #{Q_P} is therefore #{pq+qp=2B_\wedge(p,q)}, with
the same generator-square convention as #{Q_\kappa(x)=\kappa(x,x)}.}

\p{This restriction of #{B_\wedge} to #{P} is nondegenerate.
Choose a compact real form. Its Killing form is negative definite;
on exterior degree #{k}, the determinant pairing is #{(-1)^k}
times a positive definite real form. The subspace #{P} is graded
and preserved by compact conjugation, so the determinant pairing
and its degreewise sign multiple #{B_\wedge} are nondegenerate
there. This also explains the normalization change from
\citet{Section 11.3, p. 260}{meinrenken2013clifford}, which starts
with a positive compact form. Replacing that form by its negative
preserves the graded orthogonal subspaces while changing the indicated
degree signs.}
